---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const reasons = [
  {
    chip: '01',
    title: 'Transport ≠ trust framework.',
    body: 'OpenKarta is not "another way to call a tool." It is eight behavioural commitments — signed quote tokens, idempotent checkout, closed-enum errors, conformance badges, registry-of-merchants. MCP gives you none of those. Building on MCP means re-implementing every primitive on top of tools/list + tools/call — JSON-RPC-over-stdio with extra steps, coupled to a still-moving protocol surface.',
  },
  {
    chip: '02',
    title: 'MCP is LLM-client-bound. Your audience isn\u2019t.',
    body: 'OpenKarta v1 has four consumer surfaces: web app, mobile, server-side automation, and LLM agents. MCP only serves the last one. HTTP is the universal substrate every other surface speaks natively. Reverse-layering ("MCP everywhere, HTTP via shim") makes the web app speak JSON-RPC over stdio to fetch a product list. Absurd.',
  },
  {
    chip: '03',
    title: 'Server-per-merchant. Commerce needs a many-merchant registry.',
    body: 'MCP discovery is "the user/host has configured this server." There is no neutral registry, no fan-out search, no signed badge re-verified weekly. A pure-MCP world looks like every Claude Desktop user pasting JSON config for each brand they want to shop from. That is not a protocol — that is a directory problem.',
  },
  {
    chip: '04',
    title: 'You already decoupled from one vendor\u2019s roadmap.',
    body: 'OpenKarta dropped @anthropic-ai/sdk for chat/completions explicitly to escape vendor lock-in. Adopting MCP-as-substrate re-couples to the same ecosystem through a different door. Same vendor\u2019s tail, opposite end.',
  },
  {
    chip: '05',
    title: 'Merchant adoption math doesn\u2019t favor MCP.',
    body: '"Add a few HTTPS routes to your existing API" is easier than "stand up an MCP server" for a hotel chain or D2C brand whose ops team already runs HTTP services. MCP is easier for LLM tool authors, not for merchants. The spec target is the merchant.',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Why not MCP — OpenKarta" activeNav={null} description="The technical case for why OpenKarta uses HTTP as its substrate and MCP as a first-class adapter — not the other way around.">
  <!-- 1. Hero -->
  <section class="max-w-[1440px] mx-auto px-8 md:px-16 mt-20 mb-16 md:mb-32">
    <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase mb-8 border border-on-background/20 bg-on-background/5 text-on-background inline-block px-3 py-1">Architecture · why not MCP</div>
    <h1 class="font-hero-h1 text-on-background [font-size:clamp(40px,8vw,108px)] leading-[1.05] tracking-tight max-w-5xl">
      MCP is the right adapter. Not the right substrate.
    </h1>
    <p class="font-h2-editorial text-on-surface-variant mt-8 max-w-3xl">
      OpenKarta runs on plain HTTP — eight verbs, signed quotes, a registry of verified merchants. MCP is a brilliant transport for LLM tool calls, but it is not a trust framework, not a many-merchant directory, and not what most consumer surfaces speak. So we build the bridge, not the foundation.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 mt-12">
      <a class="bg-primary text-on-primary px-6 py-3 border border-primary hover:bg-primary-container hover:text-on-primary transition-colors duration-240" href="#architecture">See the architecture →</a>
      <a class="border border-on-background/25 bg-transparent text-on-background px-6 py-3 hover:bg-on-background/5 transition-colors duration-240" href="/why-registry">Why a registry →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Five reasons -->
  <section class="max-w-[1440px] mx-auto px-8 md:px-16 mb-16 md:mb-32">
    <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-on-surface-variant mb-3">01 · Why not the substrate</div>
    <h2 class="font-h2-editorial text-on-background mb-12 max-w-3xl border-b border-on-background/10 pb-4">Five reasons MCP isn’t the layer to build commerce on.</h2>
    <div class="space-y-px bg-on-background/10" data-ok-stagger=":scope > article">
      {reasons.map((r) => (
        <article class="border border-on-background/10 bg-surface-container-lowest p-8 md:p-10 fade-slide-up grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[120px_1fr] gap-6 md:gap-12">
          <div class="font-display-lg text-display-lg text-on-background/15 leading-none">{r.chip}</div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-h2-editorial text-on-background mb-4">{r.title}</h3>
            <p class="font-body-main text-on-surface-variant max-w-3xl">{r.body}</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Where MCP wins -->
  <section class="max-w-[1440px] mx-auto px-8 md:px-16 mb-16 md:mb-32 bg-surface-container-low py-12 md:py-20">
    <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-on-surface-variant mb-3">02 · The honest counterpoint</div>
    <h2 class="font-h2-editorial text-on-background mb-12 max-w-3xl">Where MCP genuinely wins.</h2>
    <div class="border border-primary/40 bg-primary p-8 md:p-12">
      <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-primary-container/80 mb-3">Distribution into LLM clients</div>
      <h3 class="font-h2-editorial text-on-primary mb-6 max-w-3xl">One config and your agent can shop across every OpenKarta-listed merchant.</h3>
      <p class="font-body-main text-primary-fixed max-w-3xl">A user with Claude Desktop, Cursor, Windsurf, or OpenAI’s MCP support installs a single MCP host configuration and gains commerce capability across the entire registry. That is a real adoption advantage, and we would be silly to leave it on the table. So we ship an adapter, not a re-platform.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. The architecture -->
  <section id="architecture" class="max-w-[1440px] mx-auto px-8 md:px-16 mb-16 md:mb-32 scroll-mt-32">
    <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-on-surface-variant mb-3">03 · The right architecture</div>
    <h2 class="font-h2-editorial text-on-background mb-12 max-w-3xl border-b border-on-background/10 pb-4">HTTP is the substrate. MCP is the bridge.</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_400px] gap-12 items-start">
      <div class="border border-on-background/10 bg-surface-container-lowest p-6 md:p-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <CodeBlock lang="text" filename="reception agents · 8 verbs · HTTP">{`                     ┌─ Web app                           (HTTP)
                     │
                     ├─ Mobile                            (HTTP)
                     │
Reception agents ────┼─ Server automation                 (HTTP)
   8 verbs · HTTP    │
                     └─ @openkarta/mcp-bridge ──MCP──▶  Claude · Cursor
                              │                          ChatGPT · any host
                              │
                              ▼
                  Hosted registry · signed quotes
                  conformance badges · audit trail`}</CodeBlock>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div class="border-l-2 border-primary pl-4">
          <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-primary mb-2">One bridge package</div>
          <p class="font-body-dense text-body-dense text-on-background"><span class="font-code-block">@openkarta/mcp-bridge</span> exposes the 8 verbs as MCP tools, points at the hosted registry, forwards the user’s BYO LLM key, and surfaces signed quotes as tool results.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-l-2 border-on-background/20 pl-4">
          <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-on-surface-variant mb-2">What stays HTTP</div>
          <p class="font-body-dense text-body-dense text-on-surface-variant">Conformance badges. Quote signing. Domain verification. The registry itself. Anything a non-LLM client needs to call.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-l-2 border-on-background/20 pl-4">
          <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-on-surface-variant mb-2">What merchants do</div>
          <p class="font-body-dense text-body-dense text-on-surface-variant">Nothing different. They expose 8 HTTP routes once. Every MCP host on Earth benefits via the bridge.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. Verdict -->
  <section class="max-w-[1440px] mx-auto px-8 md:px-16 mb-16 md:mb-32">
    <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-on-surface-variant mb-3">04 · The verdict</div>
    <h2 class="font-h2-editorial text-on-background mb-12 max-w-3xl border-b border-on-background/10 pb-4">HTTP first. MCP next. Convergence later, maybe.</h2>
    <div class="border border-on-background/15 bg-surface-container-lowest p-10 md:p-16">
      <blockquote class="font-h2-editorial text-on-background max-w-4xl mb-10 [font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,34px)] leading-snug">
        “HTTP is the right substrate. MCP is the right first-class adapter — and probably the highest-leverage missing piece for the developer track right now.”
      </blockquote>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-px bg-on-background/10">
        <div class="border border-on-background/10 bg-surface-container-lowest p-6 md:p-8">
          <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-on-surface-variant mb-3">Today</div>
          <p class="font-body-dense text-body-dense text-on-surface-variant">Ship <span class="font-code-block">@openkarta/mcp-bridge</span>. A few hundred lines on top of the orchestrator already in production. Every Claude / Cursor / ChatGPT user can shop the registry without editing LLM config.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border border-on-background/10 bg-surface-container-lowest p-6 md:p-8">
          <div class="font-label-caps text-label-caps uppercase text-on-surface-variant mb-3">12–18 months</div>
          <p class="font-body-dense text-body-dense text-on-surface-variant">If MCP ships its own commerce profile — signed quotes, idempotency, conformance — we contribute the eight verbs upstream. OpenKarta becomes the commerce profile of MCP. That is a merge case, not a rebuild.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. CTA -->
  <section class="max-w-[1440px] mx-auto px-8 md:px-16 mb-32">
    <div class="border border-on-background/15 bg-surface-container-lowest p-10 md:p-16 flex flex-col items-center text-center">
      <h2 class="font-h2-editorial text-on-background max-w-3xl mb-8">Build on the substrate that already won. Bridge into the one users live in.</h2>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 justify-center">
        <a class="bg-primary text-on-primary px-8 py-4 border border-primary hover:bg-primary-container hover:text-on-primary transition-colors duration-240 font-medium" href="/protocol">Read the specification</a>
        <a class="border border-on-background/25 bg-transparent text-on-background px-8 py-4 hover:bg-on-background/5 transition-colors duration-240 font-medium" href="/why-registry">Why a registry</a>
        <a class="border border-on-background/25 bg-transparent text-on-background px-8 py-4 hover:bg-on-background/5 transition-colors duration-240 font-medium" href="/developers">Developer quickstart</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
